course: dictionary
lesson: 12357
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To pick the right one, read the sentence and ask which meaning fits the clues around the word. «The town crier rang his bell and shouted the news in the market square.» The bell and the shouting tell you that a crier is a person who calls out news.
explain: The same word can mean something different in another sentence. «Our baby is a real crier at bedtime.» Here there is no bell and no news. The words real and bedtime tell you that crier means a person, especially a baby, who cries a lot.
explain: Old and new meanings can sit side by side. In a law court, a crier is an officer who makes announcements, and a court crier might call out «Silence in court!» To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have probably found the right one.
fact: A crier is a person who calls out or cries.
fact: A town crier, in olden times, walked the streets ringing a bell and shouting out news.
fact: Town criers often began by shouting «Oyez! Oyez!», which means listen up.
fact: Some towns in the UK, Canada and Australia still keep a town crier for festivals and ceremonies.
fact: A crier can also be a person, especially a baby or child, who cries a lot.
exercise: «The town crier rang his bell and shouted the news in the market square.» What is a town crier? | *A person who walks the streets shouting out news | A child who cries a lot | A bell that rings in a church tower | A shop that sells newspapers
exercise: «Our baby is a real crier at bedtime.» What does crier mean here? | A baby who is a lawyer | A baby who sleeps very well | *A baby who cries a lot | A baby who shouts news in the street
exercise: In an old law court, what did the crier do? | Judged the case and decided the punishment | Sold food to the people watching | *Made announcements | Wrote down every word the witnesses said
test: Some towns still keep a crier for festivals. Where might you see a crier today? | *At a festival or ceremony in some towns | On the moon, with a telescope | In a bedroom, at night | Inside every newspaper office
test: «Oyez! Oyez!» is a phrase a town crier might shout. What is the crier trying to do? | *Get people's attention so they will listen to the news | Say a sad goodbye forever | Ask for a cup of tea | Start a race through the streets
exam: Which meaning fits best in this sentence? «The crier stood on the steps and called, "Oyez! Oyez! The king has a new law!"» | *Someone who calls out news and announcements | A baby who cries all day | A bell that rings at noon | A lawyer who wins every case
